---
title: "MongoDB"
description: "A typed MongoDB client with Zod collection schemas and generated create, find, update, and delete methods for each collection."
---

# MongoDB

The MongoDB utility wraps the official `mongodb` driver in a small typed client. Each collection's shape is defined once with Zod in `schema.ts`, and a generator script writes typed methods for it, such as `db.findUserById` and `db.updateSession`, so queries and results are checked by TypeScript without an ORM. It lives in `src/lib/server/mongodb`, so SvelteKit keeps it out of the client bundle, and the code is copied into your project like every PlainSv item.

## Installation

Install the item, then set `MONGODB_URI` in `.env` to your connection string, including the database name. The client connects when the module is first imported.

```pm
npx remote0 add https://plainsv.com/r/mongodb.json
```

```bash title=".env"
MONGODB_URI="mongodb://localhost:27017/my-app"
```

## Files

The utility has three files, and only `schema.ts` needs editing by hand.

- Define collections in `src/lib/server/mongodb/schema.ts`
- Import `db`, `client`, `database`, and `ObjectId` from `src/lib/server/mongodb/index.ts`
- Run `src/lib/server/mongodb/generate.ts` to rewrite the collection methods in `index.ts`

## Define a collection

Add a key to `dbCollectionsSchema` with a Zod object. The key is the collection name, and every document needs a string `_id`. `DbCollectionsSchema` infers the TypeScript type of each collection from these schemas.

```ts title="src/lib/server/mongodb/schema.ts"
export const dbCollectionsSchema = {
	user: z.object({
		_id: z.string(),
		email: z.string(),
		image: z.string(),
		createdAt: z.date(),
		updatedAt: z.date(),
	}),
};
```

## Generate methods

Run the generator with Bun after you add, rename, or remove a collection. It replaces everything between the `//<COLLECTIONS>` and `//</COLLECTIONS>` comments in `index.ts`, so keep those comments in place. Format the file with Prettier afterwards.

```bash title="Terminal"
bun src/lib/server/mongodb/generate.ts
```

```pm
npx prettier --write src/lib/server/mongodb/index.ts
```

Each collection gets these methods, named after the collection, such as `user`:

- Create documents with `createUser` and `createUsers`
- Find documents with `findUser`, `findUsers`, and `findUserById`
- Update documents with `updateUser`, `updateUsers`, and `updateUserById`
- Delete documents with `deleteUser`, `deleteUsers`, and `deleteUserById`
- Run an aggregation pipeline with `userAggregate`

Collections whose name starts with `app`, such as `appData`, only get `createAppData`, `findAppData`, and `updateAppData`, since they hold single config documents.

## Query documents

Call the generated methods from server code, such as a `+page.server.ts` load function, a `+server.ts` endpoint, or a function handler. Filters and updates are typed against the collection schema, and `create` methods fill in `_id` with a new `ObjectId` string when you leave it out.

```ts title="src/routes/account/+page.server.ts"
import { db } from "#lib/server/mongodb/index.js";

export const load = async ({ locals }) => {
	const user = await db.findUserById(locals.userData.id);
	await db.updateUserById(locals.userData.id, { $set: { updatedAt: new Date() } });
	return { email: user?.email };
};
```

## Select fields

Pass `return` in the options to load only some fields. The result type narrows to the fields you pick.

```ts title="src/routes/account/+page.server.ts"
const user = await db.findUserById(id, { return: { email: true } });
```

## Raw driver

Use `database` for anything the generated methods do not cover, such as indexes, transactions, or change streams. It is the `Db` instance from the official driver, and `client` is the connected `MongoClient`.

```ts title="src/lib/server/setup.ts"
import { database } from "#lib/server/mongodb/index.js";

await database.collection("user").createIndex({ email: 1 }, { unique: true });
```

## Auth

The auth adapter in `src/lib/server/auth/db.mongodb.ts` stores users and sessions with this utility. It uses the `user` and `session` collections, so keep both in `schema.ts` when you use auth with MongoDB.

## API

```ts title="src/lib/server/mongodb/index.ts"
/** Connected MongoDB client */
const client: MongoClient;

/** Default database from `MONGODB_URI` */
const database: Db;

/** Typed methods for every collection in `dbCollectionsSchema` */
const db: { findUserById(id: string, options?: QueryOptions<"user">): Promise<DbCollectionsSchema["user"] | null> /* ... */ };
```

```ts title="src/lib/server/mongodb/schema.ts"
/** Zod schema for every collection */
const dbCollectionsSchema: Record<string, z.ZodType>;

/** TypeScript type of every collection, inferred from the schemas */
type DbCollectionsSchema = { [K in keyof typeof dbCollectionsSchema]: z.infer<(typeof dbCollectionsSchema)[K]> };
```
